@@ -358,8 +358,14 @@ If the first payment notice is mildly unclear but the agent promptly explains th
 
 If a payment notice says a link or amount will be shared shortly and the next message provides it within a few minutes, do not treat the first notice as bad CX when the customer did not have to chase or express confusion.
 
+If a customer says a payment link is not working, and the team sends or resends a payment link that the customer later uses successfully, treat the link issue as recovered friction inside a handled + good journey unless there is a separate material problem. Do not call this a contradiction merely because the visible short URL looks similar to the earlier link, has a trailing slash, or was described as a "new" link. It becomes bad only if the customer remains blocked, the link guidance creates duplicate-payment risk, or a later reminder conflicts with a visible completed payment.
+
 Payment-cycle, salary-cycle, credit/VAT, direct-debit, future-renewal, and payment-plan explanations are not wrong_info by default. If the agent gives concrete amounts, due dates, what is due now versus later, and the customer accepts the explanation, treat the journey as handled + good unless visible context proves the calculation false or the explanation leaves unresolved confusion.
 
+When judging payment reminders, match the payment purpose, amount, cycle, and obligation. A completed AED 1,743 July monthly payment does not automatically clear or contradict a separate AED 4,463 two-year visa-fee reminder. If the bot tells the customer to ignore an AED 4,463 visa-fee reminder because a different monthly payment was received, and a later human agent correctly explains the visa fee is still due, the bad experience comes from the earlier bot misguidance and repeated reminder confusion. Do not treat the later human clarification or the earlier visa-fee reminder that agrees with it as the contradiction source.
+
+When a customer says they already paid a worker's salary in advance, do not infer that the worker already collected or received that salary unless the transcript explicitly says so. Customer-side advance collection and worker-side salary release/collection can be different stages. If the bot explains that the customer's prior advance payment covered June, the new beginning-of-July deduction covers July, July salary will be released in August, and then answers that the worker can collect June salary at the beginning of July, treat the salary-cycle objective as handled. This is not a contradiction by itself because the customer's advance payment and the worker's bank collection are separate events.
+
 Automatic bank-deduction retry is a process state, not automatically an unhandled payment outcome. If the customer says funds were added, asks the company to try again, or worries about paying by link because of prior duplicate charging, classify the payment inquiry as handled when the agent explains that retry is automatic/bank-controlled, gives the retry window or trigger, tells the customer to keep funds available, and clearly says not to use the payment link now when that could duplicate the charge. A valid payment reminder before retry may be minor pressure, but the journey can remain customer_experience = good when the final guidance is safe, clear, accepted, and no contradictory payment/refund state remains.
 
 For duplicate-charge or payment/refund correction journeys, distinguish handled_status from customer_experience. If the final visible state gives a concrete usable path such as the duplicate charge was identified, a full refund is being processed, and a specific refund timeline is given, classify the payment objective as handled. Do not mark unhandled solely because earlier payment-state messages contradicted each other. However, contradictory notices such as "refund/transfer initiated", then "no refund was initiated", then "duplicate charge refund is processing" create trust risk and should usually make customer_experience = bad even when the final path is concrete.
@@ -668,11 +674,26 @@ Agent sends a payment-process notice about a one-time card payment and future di
 The initial notice could have been clearer, but the clarification addressed the plausible open questions and the customer accepted it.
 → handled + good, or handled + bad only if the clarification required repeated effort or left uncertainty
 
+## Handled + Good (advance salary deduction and worker collection timing)
+Customer says they already paid June salary in advance and asks why there is another deduction at the beginning of July. Bot explains the advance payment covered June, the July deduction covers July under the new start-of-month cycle, and July salary will be released in August. Customer then asks when the worker can collect June salary from the bank. Bot answers that June salary can be collected at the beginning of July, usually by the 1st or 2nd.
+This is handled because the duplicate-deduction concern and the worker collection timing were both answered. Do not mark contradiction or totally_unresolved merely because the customer paid June in advance; customer payment collection and worker salary release are separate stages unless the transcript explicitly says the worker already received June salary.
+-> handled + good
+
 ## Handled + Good (payment notice followed by link)
 Agent announces a one-time payment or process update and says the payment link will be shared shortly. A few minutes later, the link and amount are sent. The customer does not have to chase and shows no confusion.
 This is normal operational sequencing, not bad CX.
 -> handled + good
 
+## Handled + Good (payment link recovered after customer reports link problem)
+Customer receives a failed-bank-deduction notice, asks whether the payment was taken, and says the messages are mixed. Agent clarifies that salary transfer and customer payment are different, confirms the payment is still pending, and shares the payment link. Customer says the link is not working, the team resends or refreshes the link, and the customer later says they paid. Agent confirms payment received.
+Do not mark this as bad CX or a contradiction solely because the resent short payment URL appears similar to the earlier URL or because the agent called it a new link. The material issue was recovered once the customer received a usable payment path and payment was confirmed. Penalize only if the repeated link keeps failing, duplicate-payment risk is created, or a later payment reminder contradicts the confirmed payment.
+-> handled + good
+
+## Handled + Bad (bot says ignore separate visa-fee reminders)
+Customer pays a monthly amount and receives bot answers saying payment was received, no extra payment is needed, and AED 4,463 visa-fee reminders can be ignored. The reminders continue, the customer says they relied on the earlier advice, and a human agent later explains that AED 4,463 is a separate remaining two-year visa-fee amount due July 1. The customer agrees to pay later and the agent notes the date.
+This is handled because the final payment purpose, amount, due state, and customer's planned action are clear. The experience is bad because earlier bot answers conflated a completed monthly payment with a separate visa-fee obligation and told the customer to ignore valid reminders. The contradiction source should be the earlier bot/assistant ignore/no-extra-payment answer, not the visa-fee reminder or the human agent message that correctly says the visa fee remains due.
+-> handled + bad
+
 ## Handled + Good (brief status check then answer)
 Customer asks whether a visa transfer, payment, or process step is complete. Agent says "I'll check" or asks for a moment, then gives the direct completed/current status shortly afterward. Customer thanks the agent and does not chase.
 The brief check is minor or neutral, not enough to make the journey bad.
